<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="Xunit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\AiDe.App\AiDe.App.csproj" />

    <!--
      The GUI terminal probe, built alongside the tests so the case cannot silently skip.

      TerminalGuiHostTests starts this as a process to ask whether ConPTY delivers output to a
      host with no console — the product's own configuration. Without this reference the probe is
      simply absent on a clean clone and the test fails on a missing file, which reads as broken
      tooling rather than as the question going unasked (DC-012).

      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" because it is started, never linked.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\AiDe.App.TerminalProbe\AiDe.App.TerminalProbe.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />

    <!--
      The canvas probe, for the same reason and found the same way: a full clean followed by a
      Release-only build left P2FOCUS03 failing with "the canvas probe was not built". The message
      was honest — the probe genuinely was not there — but the cause was a missing build-order edge,
      not a broken product, and on a clean clone it reads as broken tooling rather than as the
      keyboard-trap question going unasked (DC-012).

      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" because it is started as a process, never linked.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\AiDe.App.CanvasProbe\AiDe.App.CanvasProbe.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />

    <!--
      The web-host probe, the THIRD instance of the same build-order edge, added by the node that
      needed it rather than after the same failure a third time. It renders the vendored CodeMirror
      bundle inside a real WebView2 over the virtual host mapping, which a `dotnet test` host cannot
      do (DC-014). Its absence must read as a failure, never as a skip (DC-012).

      This one was found the way the canvas one was: a Release-only build left the Debug probe
      missing and the test failed with "the web-host probe was not built" — an honest message about
      a missing edge, not about the product.

      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" because it is started as a process, never linked.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\AiDe.App.WebHostProbe\AiDe.App.WebHostProbe.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />

    <!--
      The composer probe, the FOURTH instance of the same build-order edge (DC-023), and the first
      one where the stale binary DID NOT announce itself. The other three were found by the probe
      being ABSENT, which fails with an honest message. This one was found by the probe being
      PRESENT AND OLD: on the merge of feature/provider-config the test ran a probe built 32 minutes
      earlier, before the three handshake fixes that commit landed, so
      TheHandshakePushesExactlyOneHostInitPerMountAndFieldValuesSurviveIt asserted 2 and measured 0.
      The identical tree passed in the worktree that had built the probe and failed in the checkout
      that had not — same commit, same machine, opposite results, and nothing in the failure named
      the binary.

      That is the shape worth remembering: a missing probe fails loudly, a STALE probe fails as a
      product defect. Every out-of-process probe this project drives belongs here on the day it is
      written, not after the failure it causes.

      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" because it is started as a process, never linked.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\AiDe.App.ComposerProbe\AiDe.App.ComposerProbe.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />

    <!--
      The contrast probe, the FIFTH instance of the same build-order edge (DC-023), added on the
      day it was written. It boots the real AiDe.App.App out of process and walks every text
      pairing the shell composes; ShellContrastCensusTests reads its report. An in-process
      census was tried first and could not coexist with the suite: WPF allows one Application
      per process, and shutting it down unregistered the pack: URI scheme for the twelve tests
      that ran after it (DC-008).

      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" because it is started as a process, never linked.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\AiDe.App.ContrastProbe\AiDe.App.ContrastProbe.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Shared by both test projects: every method added to IWorkspaceQueries used to break four
         hand-written stubs across two assemblies. Linked rather than packaged, because a project
         for forty lines is more machinery than the churn it removes. -->
    <Compile Include="..\Shared\FakeWorkspaceQueries.cs" Link="FakeWorkspaceQueries.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>